Subject: Re: Memory troubles, continued

Scott Manley <spm@star.arm.ac.uk> writes:
> So to say the least I'm a bit ticked off, everyone on this list always reporst 
> that UDB's  use just bog standard 36 bit true parity 72 pin 70ns ram. And 
> indeed that's the spec fo the 12 meg simms alreadyu in my machine. So 
> evidently there's something else which people should be aware of when trying 
> to upgrade their boxes. 
> 
> Can any9one explain this? An I don't want anyone to suggest simply that 
> Alpha's do more with their memory than Intel Boxes without giving a technical 
> explanation.

I heard that modern SIMMs with few chips are differently organized
than older ones. New ones might use 2k or 4k refresh cycles, e.g. they
need 11 or 12 address lines for refresh. Older chipsets might only
support 1k refresh.

Of course I don't know if that's true in your case. 

BTW, something similar occured when "standard" 9 bit SIMMs with 9
chips got replaced with 3 chip SIMMs. That was a problem for some old
386 boards.

Martin

